WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.260
Imagine for a second, right, that you just bought this million-dollar life insurance policy on your house.

00:05.380 --> 00:05.600
Okay.

00:05.860 --> 00:07.140
You feel completely secure.

00:07.760 --> 00:08.840
The paperwork is signed.

00:09.060 --> 00:12.000
But then, you know, you look closely at the fine print.

00:12.100 --> 00:12.900
Always a bad sign.

00:13.060 --> 00:13.320
Right.

00:14.020 --> 00:21.280
And you realize the insurance company calculated your risk of a house fire using environmental data from literally the Ice Age.

00:21.540 --> 00:22.280
Oh, wow.

00:22.280 --> 00:27.120
Yeah, they completely ignored the fact that your neighborhood is currently in a historic drought.

00:27.120 --> 00:32.980
And, like, someone next door just started a fireworks manufacturing business in their garage.

00:33.180 --> 00:34.240
Yeah, that's not great.

00:34.320 --> 00:37.660
You would panic because your insurance is basically an illusion at that point.

00:37.700 --> 00:39.260
It is a total mathematical fiction.

00:40.000 --> 00:42.040
I mean, you have a piece of paper that says you're protected.

00:42.660 --> 00:47.740
But the physical reality surrounding your house guarantees that you're absolutely not.

00:47.840 --> 00:48.280
Exactly.

00:48.280 --> 00:52.360
Now, I want you, the listener, to scale that panic up to a planetary level.

00:52.360 --> 00:59.040
Because that scenario, that is exactly what is happening right now with billions of dollars in global carbon markets.

00:59.480 --> 00:59.580
Yeah.

00:59.700 --> 01:04.840
It really is the defining architectural flaw of how we govern planetary health right now.

01:05.380 --> 01:12.920
We are trading biological resilience using these actuarial tables that are, frankly, entirely detached from physical reality.

01:12.920 --> 01:20.200
I have to say, the source material we are looking at for today's deep dive, it completely rewired how I see the natural world.

01:20.300 --> 01:20.760
Same here.

01:20.900 --> 01:22.840
And how I see the financial world, for that matter.

01:22.880 --> 01:23.100
Okay.

01:23.360 --> 01:27.360
We are pulling apart this single monumental theoretical paper.

01:27.520 --> 01:29.820
It's titled Proxy Permanence Failure.

01:30.080 --> 01:33.520
And it's written by an independent researcher known as Fliction.

01:33.820 --> 01:36.300
And what Fliction does here is just mind-bending.

01:36.540 --> 01:37.200
It really is.

01:37.300 --> 01:40.520
They take three completely distinct empirical studies.

01:40.520 --> 01:48.040
So there's one on California wildfires, one on global biodiversity, and one on the microscopic chemistry of the ocean in Japan.

01:48.180 --> 01:48.380
Right.

01:48.540 --> 01:54.460
And they run all of that through the mathematics of artificial intelligence and advanced geometry, which sounds crazy, but it works.

01:54.720 --> 01:55.460
It does work.

01:55.540 --> 02:03.320
And what's fascinating here is how the paper unites completely different scientific fields to diagnose a single systemic failure.

02:03.320 --> 02:06.920
They do all of that to prove one terrifying point.

02:07.060 --> 02:07.380
Which is one.

02:07.460 --> 02:11.700
That the fundamental premise of global carbon markets is structurally broken.

02:11.980 --> 02:12.140
Yeah.

02:12.320 --> 02:26.800
The whole idea that you can point to a forest, mint a carbon credit, and trade it on Wall Street as a durable, unchanging asset, like it's a fungible stock of carbon that's the same everywhere, is a total illusion.

02:26.800 --> 02:30.480
So our entire mission today is to decode that illusion.

02:30.940 --> 02:42.620
We're going to look under the hood of these so-called nature-based climate solutions and figure out the exact mechanisms of why our financial spreadsheets are divorcing from ecological reality.

02:42.780 --> 02:43.920
It's a massive decoupling.

02:44.140 --> 02:53.600
We'll explore the math, the biology, and ultimately this radical new mathematical framework of Fliction proposes to actually fix the system before it just entirely collapses.

02:53.760 --> 02:54.600
A lot of ground to cover.

02:54.780 --> 02:55.120
It is.

02:55.120 --> 02:57.720
So let's jump right into the fire, literally.

02:58.260 --> 03:03.040
Let's talk about the first empirical failure mode in the paper, which they call the actuarial illusion.

03:03.380 --> 03:03.600
Right.

03:03.840 --> 03:10.360
So to understand this failure, we really have to understand the baseline of how a carbon offset actually works today.

03:10.500 --> 03:11.300
Walk us through it.

03:11.580 --> 03:13.500
Let's say you are an airline, right?

03:13.900 --> 03:17.620
And you want to claim that your flights are carbon neutral.

03:18.480 --> 03:23.080
You can't exactly stop emitting carbon from your jet engines, so you go to a carbon broker.

03:23.160 --> 03:23.460
Okay.

03:23.460 --> 03:27.940
The broker finds a landowner, say in California, who has 1,000 acres of timber.

03:28.260 --> 03:36.860
If the landowner agrees not to chop down those trees, the broker calculates how much carbon is locked inside that wood.

03:37.000 --> 03:38.760
So they measure the physical trees.

03:38.940 --> 03:39.340
Exactly.

03:39.340 --> 03:43.620
Then they mint that saved carbon into credits, and they sell them to you, the airline.

03:43.820 --> 03:45.960
So the airline gets to balance its spreadsheet.

03:46.340 --> 03:53.300
They emitted like 10,000 tons of carbon into the air, but they bought 10,000 tons of carbon locked in a forest.

03:53.600 --> 03:54.280
Net zero.

03:54.580 --> 03:54.840
Boom.

03:54.980 --> 03:55.460
Net zero.

03:55.460 --> 03:57.680
But there is an obvious problem here.

03:58.280 --> 04:00.900
What happens if that forest burns down 10 years from now?

04:01.000 --> 04:01.240
Right.

04:01.480 --> 04:03.340
The airline already claimed the neutrality.

04:04.080 --> 04:09.080
The carbon from the jet fuel is in the atmosphere, and now the carbon for the trees is in the atmosphere, too.

04:09.480 --> 04:10.460
So the ledger is broken.

04:10.660 --> 04:10.820
Okay.

04:10.880 --> 04:11.780
Let's unpack this.

04:11.840 --> 04:14.820
Because how do the markets currently handle that risk?

04:15.020 --> 04:16.060
Forests burn, obviously.

04:16.160 --> 04:18.120
They must have a contingency plan for that, right?

04:18.520 --> 04:18.860
They do.

04:19.020 --> 04:19.920
It's called a buffer pool.

04:20.040 --> 04:20.700
A buffer pool.

04:20.700 --> 04:20.940
Yeah.

04:21.220 --> 04:25.160
It functions a lot like a reserve bank or, you know, an insurance mechanism.

04:25.920 --> 04:34.140
When that California project is minted, a certain percentage of those carbon credits, maybe 15 or 20 percent, are not sold to the airline.

04:34.300 --> 04:35.220
They hold them back.

04:35.360 --> 04:35.660
Right.

04:36.080 --> 04:39.880
They are placed into this central shared vault called the buffer pool.

04:40.600 --> 04:48.920
The theory is that if one forest randomly burns down, the regulator can simply retire an equivalent amount of credits from the buffer pool to cover the loss.

04:48.920 --> 04:51.460
So the global spreadsheet stays perfectly balanced.

04:52.120 --> 04:52.340
Okay.

04:52.420 --> 04:54.320
I mean, that sounds perfectly logical on paper.

04:54.520 --> 04:55.900
It's just basic insurance math.

04:56.120 --> 04:56.600
It is.

04:56.920 --> 05:00.980
And this is where Flixchen introduces a 2026 study by Wu et al.

05:01.380 --> 05:08.660
This team analyzed the California Air Resources Board, they call it CARB, and their specific forest offset buffer pool.

05:08.860 --> 05:09.660
And what did they find?

05:10.080 --> 05:14.120
Wu's team looked at 116 compliance offset projects in the system.

05:14.700 --> 05:16.560
And the numbers they found are catastrophic.

05:16.560 --> 05:22.280
They discovered that the CIRB buffer pool is undersized by an average factor of 6.3.

05:22.440 --> 05:23.720
Wait, a factor of 6.3?

05:23.880 --> 05:26.940
Like, not 6.3 percent, but over six times too small?

05:27.300 --> 05:27.740
Exactly.

05:28.240 --> 05:34.680
Depending on the specific sensitivity analysis in the study, it actually ranges from 2.2 to 8.0 times too small.

05:34.800 --> 05:35.700
Oh, my God.

05:35.780 --> 05:40.080
Yeah, the insurance vault is essentially empty compared to the actual risk it's supposed to be covering.

05:40.080 --> 05:48.700
How does an organization as large and, like, supposedly sophisticated as the California Air Resources Board get the math that wildly wrong?

05:48.920 --> 05:50.780
Are they just bad at counting trees?

05:51.180 --> 05:53.160
No, it's not that they're bad at counting trees.

05:53.520 --> 05:57.240
It's that their entire mathematical model is built on a fatal assumption.

05:58.040 --> 06:01.860
It's what ecologists call stationary ecological conditions.

06:02.120 --> 06:02.680
Stationary.

06:02.680 --> 06:03.020
Yeah.

06:03.760 --> 06:08.300
When the regulators built the buffer pool, they calculated the risk of fire by looking backward.

06:08.840 --> 06:14.360
They pulled historical data on wildfires, droughts, insect outbreaks from the last few decades.

06:14.520 --> 06:16.420
To figure out the average rate of disturbance.

06:16.520 --> 06:16.960
Precisely.

06:17.480 --> 06:20.000
They assumed the past is a perfect predictor of the future.

06:20.620 --> 06:22.720
They assumed the climate is a stationary system.

06:22.880 --> 06:23.060
Ah.

06:23.060 --> 06:29.320
But as we all know, because of antiprogenic climate forcing, we no longer live in a stationary system.

06:29.420 --> 06:29.640
Right.

06:29.880 --> 06:31.380
The background temperature is rising.

06:32.020 --> 06:34.200
Precipitation patterns are completely shifting.

06:34.640 --> 06:35.120
Exactly.

06:35.880 --> 06:44.240
The WU study ran these forest projects through a moderate emissions scenario, what climate modelers call SSP2 4.5.

06:44.240 --> 06:54.700
And under that scenario, the climate change we are already locked into roughly triples the probability of wildfire-driven reversals over a hundred-year period.

06:54.880 --> 06:55.660
It triples it.

06:55.700 --> 06:56.300
Triples it.

06:56.380 --> 06:58.700
So the historical data is worse than useless.

06:58.920 --> 07:00.580
It's actively misleading them.

07:00.660 --> 07:03.300
It is blinding them to the cliff they're driving over.

07:03.300 --> 07:17.780
Because the models ignore this accelerating non-stationary risk, WU's study concludes that a staggering 41% of the currently active carbon projects in California face a greater than 50% chance of going up in smoke.

07:17.880 --> 07:19.240
Over their credited lifespan.

07:19.520 --> 07:20.460
That is just why.

07:20.540 --> 07:28.160
Like I said, it's like selling someone life insurance based on actuarial tables from 1920 and just completely ignoring the fact that the person just took up juggling chainsaws.

07:28.400 --> 07:29.300
That's a great way to put it.

07:29.420 --> 07:31.920
You are ensuring a reality that no longer exists.

07:31.920 --> 07:37.840
But if almost half of these projects are essentially a coin flip for burning down, why doesn't the system just update the math?

07:37.940 --> 07:39.940
Like why not just make the buffer pool bigger?

07:40.240 --> 07:42.340
That is the multi-billion dollar question.

07:42.700 --> 07:45.840
And, you know, it's really important to separate the math from the politics here.

07:46.100 --> 07:48.680
Right, because we want to be totally impartial about the models themselves.

07:48.860 --> 07:49.260
Exactly.

07:49.800 --> 07:54.600
Fliction isn't writing a manifesto against cap-and-trade programs or carbon markets in general.

07:54.960 --> 07:57.600
They are performing an autopsy on a mathematical failure.

07:57.840 --> 08:01.180
Okay, so mathematically, why can't they just make the pool bigger?

08:01.180 --> 08:08.340
Because of the underlying geometry of how a force collapses, the current models assume that risks are linear and independent.

08:08.640 --> 08:14.460
Meaning, if one forest burns, it doesn't mean the forest 100 miles away is more likely to burn.

08:14.700 --> 08:16.740
They treat them like independent dice rolls.

08:17.020 --> 08:17.360
Right.

08:17.920 --> 08:23.020
But fliction introduces a concept to explain why this is a geometric failure.

08:23.280 --> 08:25.160
It's called the resilience ratio.

08:25.500 --> 08:26.720
Resilience ratio, okay.

08:26.720 --> 08:35.360
It's a calculation that balances an ecosystem's ability to damp down chaos-like, to recover from a hit against the rate of disturbances hitting it.

08:35.600 --> 08:39.160
When that ratio is greater than one, the ecosystem persists.

08:39.300 --> 08:43.060
So it might take damage from a fire or a drought, but it grows back.

08:43.180 --> 08:43.780
It has a buffer.

08:44.220 --> 08:44.500
Yes.

08:45.240 --> 08:50.940
But when climate forcing relentlessly increases the temperature and the drought frequency, that ratio drops.

08:50.940 --> 08:56.420
And when the resilience ratio hits exactly one, you cross a phase transition boundary.

08:56.560 --> 08:57.280
That's a tipping point.

08:57.480 --> 08:58.700
A catastrophic tipping point.

08:59.340 --> 09:02.560
Below one, the system does not degrade slowly or linearly.

09:03.020 --> 09:05.640
It enters what Flixing calls a collapse cascade.

09:06.480 --> 09:08.260
The local risks synchronize.

09:08.540 --> 09:10.040
Wait, synchronize how?

09:10.200 --> 09:14.600
Well, a prolonged drought weakens the immune system of the trees across an entire region, right?

09:14.980 --> 09:19.000
That weakness invites bark beetles, which normally would only kill a few sick trees.

09:19.000 --> 09:20.800
But now they explode in population.

09:21.080 --> 09:21.400
Exactly.

09:21.680 --> 09:22.900
They kill millions of trees.

09:23.300 --> 09:26.540
Those dead trees stand there drying out, creating an absolute ocean of fuel.

09:26.740 --> 09:30.640
And then a single lightning strike causes a megafire that wipes out a million acres.

09:30.900 --> 09:31.420
Oh, wow.

09:31.520 --> 09:33.720
So the events aren't independent dice rolls at all.

09:33.800 --> 09:35.380
They are deeply physically connected.

09:35.880 --> 09:37.280
The drought causes the beetles.

09:37.380 --> 09:38.740
The beetles cause the dead wood.

09:38.860 --> 09:40.620
The dead wood causes the megafire.

09:40.800 --> 09:41.160
Exactly.

09:41.600 --> 09:47.380
The flat rate buffer pool treats these as random, independent events that can be averaged out over a continent.

09:47.380 --> 09:52.040
But the math proves they are highly connected, compounding disasters.

09:52.600 --> 09:59.760
So the governance models drew an imaginary box of assumptions around the forest, and the physical reality of the forest has violently left that box.

10:00.080 --> 10:03.500
The spreadsheet says the carbon is safe, but the thermometer says otherwise.

10:03.780 --> 10:08.560
Okay, so we are watching forests burn from the outside because our models are stuck in the past.

10:08.760 --> 10:11.620
But here's where Flixing takes a massive turn in the paper.

10:11.860 --> 10:12.120
Right.

10:12.120 --> 10:16.620
Because they argue that climate change acting on the outside of the forest is only half the failure.

10:17.340 --> 10:22.460
To understand the full illusion of the carbon market, we have to look inside the forest itself.

10:22.840 --> 10:27.380
Which brings us perfectly to the second massive empirical failure in our carbon accounting.

10:27.500 --> 10:29.740
The total blindness to biodiversity.

10:30.080 --> 10:31.460
Yes, the invisible shield.

10:31.460 --> 10:39.220
Let's dig into this Weisskopf study from 2024, because this completely changed how I think about what a forest actually is.

10:39.340 --> 10:39.900
It's profound.

10:39.980 --> 10:46.860
We spend all this time worrying about fires and chainsaws, but Weisskopf looked at what happens when a forest simply loses its biological richness.

10:47.640 --> 10:48.400
What did they find?

10:48.400 --> 10:54.480
The findings are staggering, and they operate entirely independent of wildfires.

10:55.120 --> 11:01.340
Weisskopf's team modeled what would happen to global terrestrial carbon if we simply continue to see a decline in biodiversity.

11:01.700 --> 11:05.400
Like if insect populations drop or certain mammals disappear.

11:05.560 --> 11:05.960
Exactly.

11:06.140 --> 11:08.520
Specific fungi dying out, birds disappearing.

11:09.040 --> 11:14.120
They found that biodiversity decline alone could cause massive global carbon losses.

11:14.240 --> 11:14.880
Oh, massive.

11:14.880 --> 11:22.000
Under a highly optimistic sustainability scenario, we would lose between 7.4 and 103.1 petagrams of carbon.

11:22.240 --> 11:23.200
Oh, my goodness.

11:23.560 --> 11:29.080
And if we follow a more realistic fossil fuel development pathway, we could lose up to 146 petagrams.

11:29.240 --> 11:32.420
Okay, those numbers are huge, but let's translate them so we can actually visualize it.

11:32.580 --> 11:35.120
A petagram is a billion metric tons, right?

11:35.380 --> 11:35.700
Correct.

11:35.900 --> 11:37.740
So 146 petagrams of carbon.

11:38.440 --> 11:39.540
Let's put that in perspective.

11:39.760 --> 11:44.040
Humanity emits roughly 10 petagrams of carbon a year from burning fossil fuels.

11:44.040 --> 11:44.480
Yeah.

11:44.480 --> 11:58.360
So Weisskopf is saying that just by losing species, just by the ecosystem getting less diverse, the Earth could spontaneously hemorrhage the equivalent of 14 years worth of all human industrial emissions combined?

11:58.580 --> 11:58.800
Yes.

11:59.180 --> 12:05.100
The carbon just slips away into the atmosphere because the biological architecture holding it in place has dissolved.

12:05.260 --> 12:06.160
That is terrifying.

12:06.160 --> 12:19.680
And the really terrifying part is how our governance systems view this because the major climate simulations we use, they're called Earth System Models or ESMs, are the tools that governments and carbon markets use to predict the future.

12:19.940 --> 12:20.040
Right.

12:20.040 --> 12:23.440
And those models are functionally blind to biodiversity.

12:23.920 --> 12:29.540
But wait, how can a global climate model be blind to the species literally living in the climate?

12:29.860 --> 12:31.840
It comes down to computational history, mostly.

12:32.400 --> 12:40.200
When scientists first started building these massive global models decades ago, computers weren't powerful enough to simulate millions of individual species.

12:40.240 --> 12:40.960
Sure, that makes sense.

12:40.960 --> 12:43.680
So they created broad categories called plant functional types.

12:44.300 --> 12:52.700
Instead of modeling a specific type of oak tree that relies on a specific type of jaybird to bury its acorns, the model just sees broadleaf tree.

12:52.980 --> 12:53.540
Oh, I see.

12:53.540 --> 13:03.260
A complex, vibrant, hyper-diverse jungle is reduced mathematically to maybe 10 or 15 generic categories of vegetation.

13:03.600 --> 13:05.960
So they are literally just counting the mass of the wood.

13:06.060 --> 13:11.280
They don't care who the trees are or what kind of bugs are crawling on them or what kind of fungi are connecting their roots.

13:11.560 --> 13:11.920
Exactly.

13:12.580 --> 13:22.860
The models treat a 500-year-old hyper-diverse old-growth forest and a sterile, industrially planted pine monoculture as the exact same thing.

13:22.860 --> 13:26.040
As long as they hold the same raw tonnage of carbon today.

13:26.220 --> 13:26.660
Precisely.

13:26.720 --> 13:29.080
It's like looking at two identical buildings from the outside.

13:29.440 --> 13:34.900
But if you look at the blueprints, one building is made of reinforced steel, interlocking beams, and shock absorbers.

13:34.980 --> 13:35.180
Okay.

13:35.360 --> 13:38.420
And the other building is made of dry, uncooked pasta.

13:38.560 --> 13:39.160
I love that.

13:39.360 --> 13:43.740
The carbon ledger just looks at the scale and says, building 10,000 tons.

13:44.280 --> 13:46.840
It completely ignores the structural integrity.

13:46.840 --> 13:53.680
When a mild earthquake hits, the pasta building shatters into dust, but the ledger didn't see it coming because it only weighed the building.

13:53.980 --> 13:55.700
That is a brilliant way to visualize it.

13:56.180 --> 14:01.120
The carbon market is measuring the cargo, but it is completely ignoring the ship's hull.

14:01.340 --> 14:01.660
Right.

14:02.080 --> 14:05.780
Fliction dives deep into the empirical mathematics of this relationship.

14:06.480 --> 14:14.660
There is actually a proven scaling equation where the biomass of an ecosystem is directly proportional to its biological richness.

14:14.660 --> 14:15.100
Really?

14:15.600 --> 14:15.800
Yeah.

14:16.340 --> 14:20.260
And the paper introduces a concept here called constraint dimensionality.

14:20.400 --> 14:21.340
Constraint dimensionality.

14:21.420 --> 14:21.620
Okay.

14:21.860 --> 14:22.900
Break down the mechanism there.

14:23.120 --> 14:27.760
How does a bird or a bug act as a dimension of a constraint?

14:28.120 --> 14:32.040
Well, we usually think of biodiversity through the lens of a nature documentary.

14:32.380 --> 14:32.520
Yeah.

14:32.760 --> 14:33.260
It's beautiful.

14:33.460 --> 14:35.200
It has intrinsic moral value.

14:35.280 --> 14:35.460
Sure.

14:35.840 --> 14:41.180
But from a purely mathematical standpoint, a highly diverse forest is essentially a distributed computer.

14:41.300 --> 14:42.660
A distributed computer.

14:42.660 --> 14:42.940
Yeah.

14:42.940 --> 14:46.340
It is a biological architecture made of overlapping constraints.

14:46.780 --> 14:47.860
How does a forest compute?

14:48.020 --> 14:49.480
By processing environmental shocks.

14:50.060 --> 14:51.720
Let's go back to your pasta building analogy.

14:51.960 --> 14:52.140
Yeah.

14:52.340 --> 15:00.820
If a forest is just a monoculture like a million of the exact same species of pine tree, it only has one answer to an environmental problem.

15:00.900 --> 15:02.180
Because their genetics are identical.

15:02.440 --> 15:02.640
Right.

15:02.880 --> 15:12.240
If a new type of fungus arrives or a specific drought pattern hits, and that pine tree's genetics don't have the answer to survive it, the entire forest dies simultaneously.

15:12.240 --> 15:14.000
The pasta building shatters.

15:14.200 --> 15:14.560
Exactly.

15:14.720 --> 15:16.240
The carbon goes up into the atmosphere.

15:16.380 --> 15:16.560
Yeah.

15:16.760 --> 15:20.880
But a biodiverse forest has tens of thousands of different answers.

15:21.040 --> 15:21.260
Okay.

15:21.580 --> 15:24.660
Some trees have deep tap roots that survive drought.

15:25.080 --> 15:28.060
Other plants have shallow roots that capture light rain.

15:28.600 --> 15:32.120
Some species secrete chemicals that repel specific insects.

15:32.120 --> 15:32.920
Oh, I see.

15:32.920 --> 15:37.780
Certain fungi share nutrients across root systems when one area of the forest is stressed.

15:38.560 --> 15:41.060
Each of these species is a partially independent channel.

15:41.760 --> 15:45.200
They are mathematically constraining the ways the ecosystem can fail.

15:45.200 --> 15:52.780
So, the species are actively calculating, through their genetics and their interactions, how to keep the forest alive in real time.

15:52.920 --> 15:53.180
Yes.

15:53.740 --> 15:58.460
Fliction uses a framework called CLIO constraint, local inference on observables.

15:59.140 --> 16:02.260
The species act as local encoders of adaptive responses.

16:02.440 --> 16:03.540
That is fascinating.

16:03.540 --> 16:14.480
When an ecosystem loses biodiversity, when a species of bee dies out or a predator is removed, the ecosystem loses an entire channel of its ability to process shocks.

16:15.000 --> 16:17.800
The epistemic resolution of the forest drops.

16:18.300 --> 16:20.360
It loses the tools it needs to adapt.

16:20.360 --> 16:24.720
So, the carbon itself doesn't just spontaneously disappear because the bird went extinct.

16:24.860 --> 16:28.500
The bird goes extinct, which means a certain seed isn't spread.

16:28.940 --> 16:34.480
Which means a certain ground cover dies, which dries out the soil, which kills the trees, which releases the carbon.

16:34.780 --> 16:37.480
The architecture rots from the inside out.

16:37.560 --> 16:38.000
Exactly.

16:38.240 --> 16:42.720
And the carbon market spreadsheet has absolutely zero columns for any of this.

16:42.800 --> 16:44.480
They're just tracking the mass of the wood.

16:44.700 --> 16:49.140
They're trading an output metric while ignoring the machinery that produces the metric.

16:49.140 --> 16:57.760
And if you think this blindness is limited to trees on land, Fliction's third empirical synthesis takes us into a completely different environment.

16:58.640 --> 16:59.540
We have to look at the ocean.

16:59.900 --> 17:03.440
Right, because terrestrial forests are only a fraction of the global carbon sink.

17:03.680 --> 17:06.540
The ocean absorbs an unfathomable amount of our emissions.

17:07.160 --> 17:10.820
Which brings us to the Watanabe et al. study from 2026.

17:11.220 --> 17:14.260
This focuses on what is called blue carbon, specifically.

17:14.740 --> 17:17.900
The carbon processed by coastal ecosystems in Japan.

17:17.900 --> 17:26.520
When we talk about blue carbon, we are usually talking about macroalgae, like giant kelp or seagrass meadows or mangrove forests.

17:26.760 --> 17:27.960
The plants that grow underwater.

17:28.180 --> 17:28.400
Right.

17:28.840 --> 17:32.120
These plants pull massive amounts of CO2 out of the water to grow.

17:32.540 --> 17:35.200
But they don't just store carbon in their leaves and roots.

17:35.800 --> 17:43.520
A significant amount of the carbon they absorb is released directly into the surrounding seawater as dissolved organic carbon, or DOC.

17:43.520 --> 17:44.940
Dissolved organic carbon.

17:45.060 --> 17:47.040
So this isn't a solid log you can weigh.

17:47.160 --> 17:50.860
It's like an invisible soup of molecules suspended in the ocean.

17:51.020 --> 17:51.460
Exactly.

17:51.740 --> 17:52.360
It's a soup.

17:53.080 --> 17:55.940
And the Watanabe study made a huge breakthrough measurement here.

17:56.340 --> 18:03.800
They proved that a huge portion of this dissolved carbon, between 14 and 25 percent of it, persists in the ocean for over a century.

18:04.080 --> 18:04.480
Wow.

18:04.600 --> 18:05.340
Over a century.

18:05.560 --> 18:06.160
Over a century.

18:06.160 --> 18:07.280
It doesn't break down.

18:07.460 --> 18:08.640
It doesn't return to the atmosphere.

18:09.100 --> 18:12.040
It's referred to as recalcitrant DOC or RDOC.

18:12.320 --> 18:22.700
And to put that in scale, this invisible century-long storage represents roughly 4 to 8 percent of the entire annual net primary production of those plants.

18:23.360 --> 18:28.760
That is a massive amount of carbon just hanging out invisibly in the water for 100 years.

18:28.960 --> 18:32.640
It is doing exactly what we want carbon to do, staying out of the atmosphere.

18:32.880 --> 18:33.460
It's incredible.

18:33.460 --> 18:44.120
But, let me guess, the current blue carbon accounting frameworks, the ledgers that companies trade on, completely ignore this dissolved carbon.

18:44.260 --> 18:45.760
They absolutely ignore it.

18:45.780 --> 18:46.300
Unbelievable.

18:46.600 --> 18:51.080
If a carbon broker looks at a kelp forest, they will measure the physical mass of the kelp.

18:51.680 --> 18:59.260
They might measure the carbon buried in the sediment beneath the kelp, but they assign zero value to the dissolved carbon floating in the water.

18:59.260 --> 19:00.120
But why?

19:00.300 --> 19:03.400
If we know it stays there for a century, why wouldn't we count it?

19:03.660 --> 19:12.020
Because of the underlying assumption of how we define the word storage, standard carbon accounting operates on a very primitive physical paradigm.

19:12.080 --> 19:12.660
Meaning what?

19:12.880 --> 19:17.620
They assume that for carbon to be permanently stored, it has to be locked in a physical place.

19:17.700 --> 19:19.640
A geographical vault, basically.

19:19.760 --> 19:19.920
Yeah.

19:20.080 --> 19:20.780
A tree trunk.

19:21.160 --> 19:22.020
A layer of mud.

19:22.500 --> 19:27.120
If you can't put a GPS coordinate on it and weigh it, the market can't comprehend it.

19:27.120 --> 19:34.580
So, because the dissolved carbon is just floating around in the ocean currents, moving from place to place, the spreadsheet assumes it's gone.

19:34.760 --> 19:35.260
Exactly.

19:35.800 --> 19:40.380
But, Fliction uses this study to completely redefine what storage actually means.

19:40.400 --> 19:40.640
Okay.

19:40.860 --> 19:41.560
Think about it.

19:41.560 --> 19:49.800
Why does this dissolved carbon survive for a hundred years in an ocean filled with hungry microbes that want to eat it?

19:49.880 --> 19:50.080
Right.

19:50.260 --> 19:51.460
Microbes are everywhere.

19:51.460 --> 19:56.780
The old scientific assumption was that this recalcitrant DOC must be chemically inert.

19:57.560 --> 20:02.220
They assumed it was like microscopic plastic, just fundamentally undigestible by biology.

20:02.260 --> 20:03.120
That's not true.

20:03.280 --> 20:03.480
No.

20:03.480 --> 20:08.120
Well, the Watanabe study showed that it operates on what is called a reactivity continual.

20:08.800 --> 20:10.380
The carbon is not chemically inert.

20:10.920 --> 20:14.200
It survives because of something called dynamical inaccessibility.

20:14.480 --> 20:15.860
Here's where it gets really interesting.

20:16.240 --> 20:17.640
The dynamical inaccessibility.

20:17.860 --> 20:19.060
Break down the mechanics of that.

20:19.160 --> 20:20.340
What does that actually look like?

20:20.540 --> 20:26.420
The dissolved carbon molecules have been transformed into incredibly complex, twisted configurations.

20:26.740 --> 20:27.020
Okay.

20:27.020 --> 20:32.020
The microbes swimming around in that specific patch of ocean have enzymes.

20:32.600 --> 20:33.500
They want to eat the carbon.

20:34.140 --> 20:42.640
But the carbon is folded in such a way that it requires more energy for the microbe to break the molecular bonds than the microbe would get back from eating it.

20:42.700 --> 20:45.780
Wait, so it's an energy economics problem at the microscopic scale?

20:45.920 --> 20:46.980
It completely is.

20:46.980 --> 20:50.440
So it's not that the carbon is locked inside a titanium safe.

20:50.560 --> 20:55.840
It's more like the carbon has been scrambled into a microscopic Rubik's Cube.

20:56.040 --> 20:56.280
Yes.

20:56.280 --> 21:07.080
The microbe holds the Rubik's Cube, looks at it, realizes it would take 100 calories of Blaine Power to solve it just to get a 10-calorie reward, and says, nope, not worth it, and swims away.

21:07.160 --> 21:08.540
That is exactly the mechanism.

21:08.680 --> 21:12.300
It is an operational invisibility, not a chemical impossibility.

21:12.460 --> 21:12.700
Yeah.

21:13.020 --> 21:17.800
The carbon is shielded by its geometric complexity relative to the specific microbes around it.

21:17.820 --> 21:18.340
That's wild.

21:18.440 --> 21:22.080
In fact, the Watanabe team ran an experiment to test this.

21:22.080 --> 21:28.320
They took this century-old dissolved carbon and tried to force it to degrade in a lab.

21:28.560 --> 21:31.500
They dumped in massive amounts of extra nutrients.

21:31.860 --> 21:34.920
They introduced totally new aggressive strains of bacteria.

21:35.360 --> 21:36.820
They blasted it with sunlight.

21:36.880 --> 21:40.600
It basically threw the kitchen sink at the Rubik's Cube to see if they could break it.

21:40.680 --> 21:41.280
And the result.

21:41.520 --> 21:43.640
The RDOC barely degraded at all.

21:43.720 --> 21:45.260
The microscopic shield held.

21:45.260 --> 21:46.260
Wow.

21:46.420 --> 21:53.420
So, if adding all those nutrients and new bacteria couldn't easily break down the carbon, what does that tell us about the ecosystem itself?

21:53.540 --> 21:56.740
Like, how did the carbon get scrambled into that Rubik's Cube in the first place?

21:57.220 --> 21:59.040
That is the most fascinating part.

21:59.400 --> 22:02.860
It's driven by what ecologists call the microbial carbon pump.

22:03.100 --> 22:03.400
Okay.

22:03.640 --> 22:06.400
The degradation process itself creates the shield.

22:06.840 --> 22:10.040
When the kelp first releases the carbon, some of it is very easy to eat.

22:10.700 --> 22:11.880
Labile carbon, they call it.

22:11.900 --> 22:14.420
The microbes swarm it and devour the easy parts.

22:14.420 --> 22:19.740
But as a byproduct of their digestion, they excrete more complex, humic-like compounds.

22:19.940 --> 22:20.140
Oh.

22:20.780 --> 22:24.520
The act of eating the simple carbon produces the complex carbon.

22:24.840 --> 22:25.160
Yes.

22:25.980 --> 22:29.100
Fliction calls this recursive admissibility contraction.

22:29.360 --> 22:29.740
Wow.

22:30.180 --> 22:36.940
The ecosystem is actively, dynamically reorganizing the carbon to protect it from further degradation.

22:37.660 --> 22:40.540
The microbes are inadvertently building the Rubik's Cube.

22:40.800 --> 22:43.400
That completely shatters the idea of vault.

22:43.400 --> 22:44.160
It does.

22:44.160 --> 22:46.980
The carbon isn't hiding in a discrete physical location.

22:47.620 --> 22:51.640
The carbon is protected by an ongoing dynamic process of evasion.

22:52.200 --> 23:01.260
And because our carbon markets require a simple physical object sitting still on a map, they literally cannot perceive the reality of how the ocean stores carbon.

23:01.480 --> 23:04.000
You are hitting on the exact thesis of the paper.

23:04.480 --> 23:07.640
We are trying to measure a dynamic process using a static ruler.

23:07.640 --> 23:08.200
Okay.

23:08.300 --> 23:13.000
Let's step back because this is where Fliction brings the entire paper together into this grand unification.

23:13.260 --> 23:13.320
Right.

23:13.520 --> 23:18.120
We have terrestrial forests burning because our risk models assume the climate is stationary.

23:18.460 --> 23:24.740
We have biodiversity vanishing, which destroys the internal computation and constraint architecture of the forest.

23:24.740 --> 23:31.540
And we have marine carbon hiding in plain sight protected by microbial geometry that our spreadsheets can't comprehend.

23:31.760 --> 23:32.420
It's all connected.

23:32.420 --> 23:39.400
So Fliction weaves these three empirical failures into a single mathematical theory called proxy permanence failure.

23:39.900 --> 23:41.720
What is the unifying thread here?

23:42.240 --> 23:45.960
The unifying thread requires a complete shift in our fundamental ontology.

23:46.420 --> 23:50.420
And ontology is just a philosophical study of how we define reality, what things actually are.

23:51.100 --> 23:51.340
Okay.

23:51.340 --> 23:56.460
Fliction argues that current global governance operates on an object ontology.

23:56.820 --> 23:57.640
An object ontology.

23:57.880 --> 24:00.420
So treating things as isolated lumps of matter.

24:00.680 --> 24:01.080
Exactly.

24:01.520 --> 24:04.060
They treat a carbon credit purely as physical mass.

24:04.440 --> 24:12.080
In the paper's mathematical framework, they call this variable phi, a passive reservoir of stuff, a ton of wood, a ton of sediment.

24:12.300 --> 24:12.680
It's a dead weight.

24:12.800 --> 24:12.960
Right.

24:13.280 --> 24:19.420
And if you are operating on an object ontology, you completely ignore two other vital variables of reality.

24:19.420 --> 24:27.280
You ignore the flow of material, the nutrient cycling, the constant movement of ecology, and crucially, you ignore the configurational entropy field.

24:27.440 --> 24:27.560
Okay.

24:27.660 --> 24:28.520
The entropy field.

24:28.920 --> 24:32.240
Whenever I hear entropy, my brain automatically goes to thermodynamics.

24:32.620 --> 24:36.500
You know, chaos, disorder, a coffee cup shattering, and not being able to unshatter.

24:36.800 --> 24:38.860
How does entropy apply to a carbon market?

24:39.240 --> 24:43.840
In this framework, the entropy field is simply a mathematical measure of options.

24:44.220 --> 24:48.280
It represents the logarithmic volume of accessible future trajectories.

24:48.280 --> 24:48.480
Okay.

24:48.680 --> 24:49.280
Translate that.

24:49.420 --> 24:51.200
Accessible future trajectories.

24:51.340 --> 24:54.280
How many different ways can this ecosystem survive tomorrow?

24:54.880 --> 25:01.000
If an ecosystem has high entropy in this context, it has a massive number of open paths to stay alive.

25:01.300 --> 25:02.580
It has high elasticity.

25:02.960 --> 25:05.360
So a highly biodiverse forest.

25:05.360 --> 25:10.940
It has millions of genetic and structural pathways to absorb a shock, adapt, and keep the carbon in the ground.

25:11.140 --> 25:11.940
It has high options.

25:12.100 --> 25:12.800
And low entropy.

25:13.040 --> 25:14.920
Low entropy means the system is constrained.

25:15.320 --> 25:16.260
The options are shrinking.

25:16.260 --> 25:23.220
A monoculture pine plantation experiencing a historic drought has almost zero accessible survival trajectories.

25:23.880 --> 25:26.980
It is funneled toward a single outcome, which is collapse.

25:27.540 --> 25:32.540
So the object ontology, our current carbon market, only measures the physical mass, the phi.

25:33.040 --> 25:35.760
It completely ignores the options, the entropy field.

25:36.000 --> 25:36.220
Yes.

25:36.280 --> 25:40.020
It weighs the pasta building and ignores the fact that there are no structural beams.

25:40.220 --> 25:40.720
Precisely.

25:40.720 --> 25:45.160
And Flicksham proposes that to fix the world, we have to transition to a field ontology.

25:45.340 --> 25:46.080
A field ontology.

25:46.080 --> 25:50.520
In a field ontology, permanence is not a default state of a physical object.

25:50.960 --> 25:54.000
A piece of wood is not permanently carbon just because it exists today.

25:54.700 --> 25:57.200
Permanence is a continuously achieved outcome.

25:57.520 --> 25:58.280
Achieved by what?

25:58.480 --> 26:00.520
By the entire web of the ecosystem.

26:00.860 --> 26:01.060
Yeah.

26:01.160 --> 26:09.400
The constraint networks, the biodiversity, the microbial pumps, they all actively damp down chaos and maintain that carbon against the forces of decay.

26:09.400 --> 26:11.640
So permanence is a verb, not a noun.

26:11.940 --> 26:14.620
It is something the forest does, not something the forest is.

26:14.740 --> 26:16.080
That is the core insight.

26:16.500 --> 26:19.000
And this explains the mechanism of proxy permanence failure.

26:19.280 --> 26:22.780
The proxy is the carbon credit, the ledger, the spreadsheet.

26:23.460 --> 26:28.700
The governance models draw an imaginary boundary, an admissibility manifold, they call it, around their assumptions.

26:28.960 --> 26:29.300
Right.

26:29.460 --> 26:34.940
Inside their imaginary box, the climate is stable, biology is simple, and storage is a physical vault.

26:35.500 --> 26:37.920
But the real physical world is a dynamic field.

26:37.920 --> 26:44.320
When the real world heats up, or loses species, its physical trajectory leaves that imaginary box.

26:44.440 --> 26:46.060
The reality diverges from the map.

26:46.400 --> 26:47.800
But the map doesn't know it.

26:48.100 --> 26:51.300
The symbolic projection, the ledger, looks perfectly fine.

26:51.680 --> 26:54.760
The corporation claims net zero on their annual report.

26:54.920 --> 27:01.000
But the causal, physical reality underneath that ledger is actively decaying into the atmosphere.

27:01.800 --> 27:03.140
The proxy has failed.

27:03.780 --> 27:06.020
The math here is incredibly elegant.

27:06.020 --> 27:08.120
It perfectly describes the failure.

27:08.560 --> 27:11.860
But I have to push back because we live in a real world with real economies, right?

27:12.180 --> 27:12.280
Sure.

27:12.480 --> 27:21.320
For a global financial market to function, for billions of dollars to move between corporations and conservation projects, you need simple, fungible units.

27:21.740 --> 27:25.760
You need to know that one ton of carbon equals one ton of carbon.

27:25.960 --> 27:26.140
True.

27:26.140 --> 27:33.120
You cannot walk onto the floor of the New York Stock Exchange and say, I'd like to trade 10 shares of a complex adaptive field system.

27:33.200 --> 27:35.600
The brokers would laugh you out of the room.

27:36.400 --> 27:40.500
Is Flixian essentially saying that carbon markets are inherently impossible to run?

27:40.800 --> 27:43.080
That is the critical tension of the entire paper.

27:43.520 --> 27:45.440
Flixian is not saying markets are impossible.

27:45.440 --> 27:50.580
They are saying the current mathematics we use to underpin the market makes it impossible to sustain.

27:50.940 --> 28:02.000
The market is currently built on binary actuarial permanence, which is the naive idea that the carbon is either 100% safe in a vault or it is 100% gone.

28:03.000 --> 28:08.980
Flixian says we must transition the financial architecture to a first passage survival probability.

28:09.300 --> 28:11.100
First passage survival probability.

28:11.780 --> 28:14.620
What does that actually mean for the person buying the credit?

28:14.620 --> 28:21.680
It means we must treat carbon credits as a continuous, updating calculation of risk over time.

28:22.260 --> 28:23.900
You are not trading an isolated object.

28:24.280 --> 28:25.660
You are trading a probability.

28:26.220 --> 28:27.280
How does that look in practice?

28:27.740 --> 28:28.840
Let's say you have two projects.

28:29.260 --> 28:34.160
Project A is a hyper-diverse, old-growth forest in a region with historically stable rainfall.

28:34.940 --> 28:41.780
Project B is a sterile, single-species timber farm planted in an area facing severe climate-induced drought.

28:41.780 --> 28:48.740
Under today's rules, a ton of carbon from Project A costs the exact same and acts the same as a ton from Project B.

28:48.740 --> 28:50.380
They are completely fungible.

28:50.500 --> 28:52.060
Under Flixian's map, they are not.

28:52.540 --> 28:58.060
The market can still trade them, but their financial value must dynamically reflect their continuous resilience.

28:58.400 --> 29:02.140
Project A has a massive volume of survival trajectories.

29:02.700 --> 29:05.640
Its probability of surviving the next 100 years is high.

29:06.600 --> 29:08.900
Project B has almost no survival trajectories.

29:09.020 --> 29:10.520
Its probability of collapse is high.

29:10.520 --> 29:15.560
Therefore, Project A is mathematically, financially, and ecologically more valuable.

29:16.140 --> 29:20.280
You have to price in the complexity of the field, not just the mass of the object.

29:20.620 --> 29:23.940
We have to price the structural integrity of the building, not just the weight of the concrete.

29:24.240 --> 29:24.680
Exactly.

29:24.900 --> 29:33.820
And this idea that a seemingly invisible, complex, variable like biodiversity actually controls the entire fate of a massive physical system,

29:34.280 --> 29:36.500
this is where Flixian takes a massive left turn.

29:36.620 --> 29:37.580
Yeah, this part is wild.

29:37.580 --> 29:40.120
Because they argue this isn't just an ecological quirk.

29:40.260 --> 29:42.940
This is a universal law of complex systems.

29:43.440 --> 29:47.440
And to prove it, they step completely outside of biology and look at artificial intelligence.

29:47.620 --> 29:50.300
This is where the synthesis becomes truly breathtaking.

29:50.920 --> 29:53.640
Flixian brings in a 2026 study by Wang et al.

29:54.200 --> 29:56.380
Concerning large language models, or LLMs.

29:56.920 --> 30:00.600
These are the massive AI neural networks that power things like ChatGPT.

30:00.740 --> 30:05.900
Okay, but what could a silicon neural network possibly have in common with a burning forest in California?

30:05.900 --> 30:08.040
Everything, mathematically speaking.

30:08.840 --> 30:11.800
The Wang study analyzed the architecture of these AI models,

30:12.360 --> 30:17.680
specifically focusing on something called scale vectors within the normalization layers of the network.

30:17.980 --> 30:21.580
Okay, let's break down the mechanism there for anyone who doesn't build AI for a living.

30:21.580 --> 30:25.380
What is a normalization layer, and what is a scale vector?

30:26.000 --> 30:32.460
So, when an AI is training, when it is reading billions of words and trying to learn the patterns of human language,

30:32.680 --> 30:35.360
it uses an algorithm called gradient descent.

30:35.780 --> 30:42.980
It is constantly tweaking billions of internal numbers, called parameters, to get better at predicting the next word.

30:43.220 --> 30:43.480
Right.

30:43.480 --> 30:48.180
But during this massive mathematical process, the numbers can get wildly out of balance.

30:48.720 --> 30:52.900
Some numbers get too huge, some get too microscopic, and the network essentially breaks.

30:53.280 --> 30:55.080
The learning process spirals out of control.

30:55.220 --> 30:57.900
Right. So, engineers insert normalization layers.

30:58.060 --> 30:59.700
Think of them as mathematical shock absorbers.

31:00.000 --> 31:02.660
They reset the numbers at each step so the network stays stable.

31:03.140 --> 31:07.220
And inside these shock absorbers are tiny parameters called scale vectors.

31:07.660 --> 31:09.860
They act like microscopic adjustment dials.

31:09.900 --> 31:11.300
How microscopic are we talking?

31:11.300 --> 31:15.720
The Wang study looked at a 1 billion parameter AI model.

31:16.180 --> 31:23.520
In that model, the scale vectors make up roughly 7.84 times 10 to the negative 5th of the total parameters.

31:23.660 --> 31:24.500
Let me translate that.

31:24.680 --> 31:28.460
That is less than one hundredth of 1% of the AI's architecture.

31:29.040 --> 31:30.680
It is functionally negligible.

31:30.820 --> 31:31.940
It is entirely negligible.

31:32.220 --> 31:37.440
In fact, Wang's team proved that these scale vectors are mathematically expressively redundant.

31:37.620 --> 31:37.880
Meaning?

31:37.880 --> 31:40.520
That means, on paper, you don't even need them.

31:40.520 --> 31:48.820
You could theoretically fold their multiplication logic into the adjacent layers of the network without losing a single drop of mathematical representation.

31:49.620 --> 31:51.880
The AI should function exactly the same without them.

31:52.580 --> 31:54.420
So, they look completely useless on the spreadsheet.

31:54.700 --> 31:55.080
Exactly.

31:55.640 --> 31:57.200
But here's the profound catch.

31:57.620 --> 32:04.980
When Wang's team actually removed these tiny, mathematically redundant scale vectors and tried to train the AI, the entire system collapsed.

32:05.160 --> 32:05.560
Really?

32:05.560 --> 32:06.000
Yeah.

32:06.420 --> 32:07.860
The AI suffered terminal loss.

32:08.180 --> 32:10.440
It experienced massive drops in token efficiency.

32:10.980 --> 32:12.920
The neural network basically forgot how to learn.

32:13.260 --> 32:13.440
Wait.

32:13.500 --> 32:18.980
So, if I'm looking at this parallel, the AI has these microscopic, seemingly redundant vectors.

32:19.760 --> 32:25.900
The forest has a few rare species of fungi or insects that make up a microscopic fraction of the forest's total mass.

32:25.900 --> 32:34.040
Are you saying the math governing a computer's ability to learn language is the exact same math governing a forest's ability to survive a drought?

32:34.040 --> 32:35.620
I am saying exactly that.

32:36.180 --> 32:43.540
Both the scale vectors in the AI and the biodiversity in the forest follow what Flixson calls the negligible but constitutive pattern.

32:44.300 --> 32:47.380
By sheer mass or parameter count, they appear negligible.

32:47.980 --> 32:50.040
But dynamically, they are constitutive.

32:50.340 --> 32:52.400
They constitute the survival of the system.

32:52.500 --> 32:53.300
They are the architecture.

32:53.300 --> 32:58.240
In the AI, the tiny scale vector acts as a state-dependent pre-conditioner.

32:58.600 --> 33:04.640
It subtly shapes the geometry of how the AI learns at every single step, preventing a gradient descent from spiraling into chaos.

33:04.760 --> 33:04.960
Okay.

33:05.300 --> 33:15.680
In the forest, the rare fungi or the specific pollinator subtly shapes the ecosystem's response to daily stress, preventing environmental disturbances from spiraling into a collapsed cascade.

33:15.680 --> 33:18.600
It's like the steering wheel on a massive cargo ship.

33:18.960 --> 33:24.160
If you weigh the cargo ship, it is hundreds of thousands of tons of steel and shipping containers.

33:24.660 --> 33:27.780
The steering wheel weighs maybe 10 pounds.

33:28.080 --> 33:28.180
Right.

33:28.680 --> 33:32.000
Mathematically, by mass, the steering wheel is entirely redundant.

33:32.240 --> 33:33.220
It is a rounding error.

33:33.840 --> 33:43.240
But if the spreadsheet optimizer comes along and says, we need to cut weight, rip out this 10-pound wheel, it's negligible, the ship is going to crash into a reef and sink.

33:43.400 --> 33:44.340
That is it exactly.

33:44.340 --> 33:55.400
The output metric, the total weight of the ship or the total parameter count of the AI or the total carbon tonnage of the forest, is an illusion without the underlying architecture spearing it.

33:55.640 --> 33:55.960
Wow.

33:56.240 --> 34:07.660
Anytime you try to govern a complex system using only an output metric and you discard the structural constraints because they look negligible on paper, the system is mathematically guaranteed to eventually collapse when conditions change.

34:07.780 --> 34:08.340
That is profound.

34:08.340 --> 34:14.680
We are optimizing for the cargo and throwing away the steering wheel across our entire planetary governance system.

34:14.760 --> 34:15.480
It's a scary thought.

34:15.760 --> 34:19.320
But diagnosing this as a philosophical concept isn't enough for affliction.

34:19.700 --> 34:21.640
